WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
Tor is best understood as privacy infrastructure that changes what different observers can connect.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:13.960
A local network or provider may see a connection to Tor, but should not see the final destination inside a normal circuit.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.900
A destination website may see a Tor exit relay, but not the user's original network address.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.960
Relays have limited roles: entry, middle, and exit positions each see different pieces.

5
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.380
This separation is useful because no ordinary relay should have the whole story.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.140
Tor Browser matters because the browser is part of the privacy boundary.

7
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.200
It is configured to reduce fingerprinting and to make users look more similar to each other.

8
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:49.220
Keeping the browser updated, avoiding extra extensions, respecting security levels, and separating personal accounts from private sessions all support the network protection.

9
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:57.880
Tor can hide an address from a destination, but it cannot hide a login, a phone number, a reused username, or a recognizable habit that the user provides.

10
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.480
Exit relays and HTTPS define another important boundary.

11
00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.740
When traffic leaves Tor for an ordinary website, the exit relay is the edge of Tor transport.

12
00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.680
HTTPS protects content between the browser and the destination beyond that edge.

13
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:21.580
Onion services change the path by keeping the connection inside Tor, which avoids ordinary exit relays for those services.

14
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:31.920
Bridges and pluggable transports help with access when direct Tor connections are blocked, but they do not remove the usual browser, account, file, or device risks.

15
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.760
The course also emphasized responsibility.

16
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:42.860
Tor is appropriate for lawful privacy, research, journalism, access to information, and security awareness.

17
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.360
It should not be treated as permission to ignore rules, harm other people, or trust unknown services.

18
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:57.220
A privacy tool is strongest when paired with consent, accurate expectations, and careful choices about what information to share.

19
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.740
The practical checklist is short.

20
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.680
First, identify the purpose: reading, research, access, or communication.

21
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:15.700
Second, name the observers: local network, provider, destination site, account provider, device owner, or someone nearby.

22
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:27.960
Third, choose behavior that matches the risk: avoid personal logins, avoid unnecessary downloads, verify onion addresses, prefer HTTPS, and keep Tor Browser ordinary.

23
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:36.540
Fourth, know the limits: Tor is not invisibility, malware protection, legal advice, or a fix for unsafe behavior.

24
00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.940
A responsible Tor user treats privacy as a set of decisions, not a button.

25
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:48.680
Tor can be a strong tool for lawful privacy, access, journalism, research, and security awareness.

26
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:57.340
Its value comes from matching the tool to the threat model and remembering that people, devices, websites, and local environments still matter.

27
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:06.400
If you leave this course with one habit, make it this: before using Tor, ask what you are protecting, from whom, and what you must not reveal yourself.
